hi there steve kaufman here today i want  to talk about um accents  it's a subject that comes up all the  time which accent should we learn does  it matter for example in spanish in  english and french and  in all languages there are different  accents which accent should we learn  does it matter  things of this nature  it comes up to some extent because i'm  going to latin america i had a  conversation and we were talking about  different accents you know argentinian  mexican spanish and of course the same  is true in english we have you know  american north american southern  scottish australian  what about all this stuff before i get  into that though  i just want to confirm again that you  know i'm going to be in south america  and i'm happy to have meetups or meet  with people talk about language learning  but i'm going to be traveling with my  wife and another couple so the best of  course is if people there who want to  get together organize something let me  know and then we can find something that  works  the dates i'm going to put them in i'm  going to put a link in fact in the  description box to a thread at our forum  at link lingq  where i ask people to let me know if  they want to get together and the dates  are buenos aires basically what's  available is october 3 or 4  or october 8 9 10 in buenos aires in rio  it's october 13 to 17. salvador october  18 and 19 and lima october 26 through  28th so check the link and let me know  if you want to get together  accents  it also came up because apparently  there's a dispute not a dispute but  there are  uh teachers who are non-native speaking  teachers mostly of english who feel that  they are somehow treated unfairly  and uh  and so then there was a comment about is  there any evidence that if a teacher has  an accent that it could be that it could  adversely affect the learner  or if a non-native speaking teacher of  english or any language makes a mistake  would that adversely affect the learner  so  kind of throwing these things all  together  uh i think that any one teacher  any one source of audio or text any  mistake has very little impact over the  course of your learning people get all  bent out of shape because there's some  incorrect uh you know grammar in some  text somewhere that a learner is using  it doesn't really matter in my opinion  because i know in my own experience i'm  i you know i'm exposed to so much  material so many different people so  many and even native speakers make  mistakes  so that in the end it doesn't matter as  long as you do enough listening and  reading it won't matter much and and  when we deal with the issue of accents  um  you know i i must admit that when i  learn another language initially i'm not  that aware of different accents i'm  focused on words and meaning  and  many learners i would think of spanish  in an initial period have trouble  telling whether it's from spain or from  mexico or from colombia or whatever and  that in time they become more aware of  these differences in pronunciation  they may find some people easier to  understand than others it might be how  quickly they speak or whatever but then  in time  if a person really wants to speak he's  going to live in colombia he wants to  speak like a colombian wants to  understand the colombians then at some  point it's probably a good idea to focus  on  the accent  that is most useful for you if you're  going to be working in quebec then you  should probably focus on making sure you  understand the accent in quebec and  probably you want to sound a little bit  more like a quebecer because that will  be more  you know um  sympathetic from the perspective of the  people you're dealing with so it really  depends on what your needs are  uh  you know and so therefore at one at some  point you you probably should focus on  on one form  one accent and try to really get into  the rhythm and the intonation and the  pronunciation of that accent but that  doesn't mean that you shouldn't listen  to other accents  because uh you know acquiring words and  acquiring a familiarity with the  language is kind of the basic task  so that uh  and and plus you should even if you're  going to be living in argentina you  should be able to understand people from  spain or chile  if you're going to be living in  you know australia you should still be  able to understand people from scotland  so it's useful to also expose yourself  to these other accents in my view and  certainly in the beginning i don't think  it matters very much but at some point  as a strategy  you may say okay i want to focus on  this form of the language because that's  where i'm going to be and that's what's  going to be most useful for me um  i'll just touching briefly on this idea  of non-native speaking teachers i mean  there is  people have prejudices  so i i'm sure there are situations where  a school would rather  like in japan  that they might prefer to have a  dutchman with his dutch accent as  opposed to a native speaker who looks  asian say an asian canadian which is of  course extremely unfair and and and  demonstrates prejudice on the part of  the school but maybe they feel i'm  learning english that's language of the  white people therefore i want a teacher  who's of european origin which of course  is silly however  you know if you're learning say chinese  uh would you rather learn from someone  who looks european  or someone who looks  chinese  uh  now of course if they both if they are  both non-native speakers personally i  would make no difference i i prefer to  learn from native speakers  i find it more motivating to learn from  native speakers because to me the  learning process is largely listening  and reading and any questions i have on  grammar or usage i can look them up and  then i go back to doing my listening and  reading  and then at some point i want to engage  people in conversation and it's more  motivating for me to be doing this with  a native speaker  but in a classroom where you have a  teacher who's trying to encourage people  motivate them stimulate them inspire  them help them pick them up when they  feel frustrated  i don't think it really matters whether  it's a native speaker and not a speaker  i don't think these things matter i  think  for the independent learner we should  respect the choice of the independent  learner like me i just find that if i  get the language from a native speaker  that to me is just more credible more  motivating more credible  so i would only listen to  audio  with a native speaker that's what turns  me on  i might consume content written by a  non-native speaker because i'm  interested in the subject matter but as  a deliberate choice in language learning  i would prefer the native speaker but  that's not to say that a non-native  speaker can't be very competent  in the language and we have many  examples of non-native speakers who use  say english better than many native  speakers i mean the one example that  pops to mind is of course joseph joseph  conrad who was a phenomenal  author in the english language i know  apparently had a polish uh accent until  the end of his days  so i mean it's it's the language  i can't immediately think of them but  there are well samuel beckett who is an  irishman he wrote in french  so anyone can learn to use a language  very well and theoretically can teach  the language as well as say a native  speaker but i think we also should  respect  the motivation and the preferences of  the learner so there you have it i  what i did today was i talked briefly  about my uh trip to south america  please check the link if you're  interested in participating in some kind  of a gathering in one of those cities  please come to our forum at link you'll  see a link to link  i talked about you know accents and why  i think people make too much of a of a  deal about it when they're starting out  whereas at a later stage they should  probably focus on the the form of the  language that's going to be most useful  for them and then i touched briefly on  this controversy surrounding non-native  speakers of the language so a bit of a  ramble  and i hope to see some of you in south  america bye for now  [Music]  you
 

